/-!
# `Work.K3.Defs` — vocabulary for the K3 L-link (Lin–Shinder, Theorem `thm:K3`)

This is the base module of the **K3 area** of `annals_motivic_invariants`, i.e. the `n ≥ 4` case
of Theorem 1.2 (2) of the challenge: for every subfield `𝕜 ⊆ ℂ`, `Crₙ(𝕜)` is not generated by
pseudo-regularizable elements.

The geometric input is the L-link of Lin–Shinder, `Motivic invariants of birational maps`
(Annals **199** (1), 2024), Theorem `thm:K3` (§ *K3 surfaces of degree 12*):

```
        T
      /   \
 Bl_{S_0}  Bl_{S'_0}
   /          \
 S_0 ⊂ ℙ⁴ --ψ--> ℙ⁴ ⊃ S'_0
```

with `S`, `S'` non-isomorphic K3 surfaces of degree 12 and geometric Picard rank one, `S'` the
unique Fourier–Mukai partner of `S`, and `c(ψ_𝕜) = [S_𝕜 × ℙ¹] − [S'_𝕜 × ℙ¹] ≠ 0`.

## What this module provides

* the ambient scheme `Affine4 𝕜` of the Cremona group `Cr 𝕜 4`, and the **affine transport**
  `transport ε ψ δ = ε ∘ ψ ∘ δ` of a link to `𝔸⁴`, together with the additivity of the invariant
  coefficient along it (`transportCr`, `cInv_transport_eq`, `cInv_transport_eq_of_eq_zero`) —
  the `n = 4` analogue of `Work.LLink.transport`, proved here;
* the vocabulary used by the rest of the area: stable birationality (`StablyBirational`,
  `prodOver`), isomorphisms over a base field (`IsoOver`), and the *named geometric predicates*
  `IsK3Degree12`, `PicardRankOneGeom`, `IsKTrivial`, which stand for geometric properties of
  smooth projective varieties that Mathlib cannot currently express (no smoothness, no
  projectivity, no canonical bundle, no Néron–Severi group, no intersection numbers).  They are
  *hypotheses*, never proved here, and are used only to state the named inputs of
  `Submission/Work/K3/HL.lean`.

## Conventions

* `𝕜` is a field; `Spec (.of 𝕜)` is the base scheme; `X.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))` the base structure.
* `exDiv`, `excCount`, `cInv`, `ResEquiv`, `ResAlgEquiv` are those of `Work.Motivic.Defs`
  (Part A of this project).
* The two centres of the link are named `S` and `S'` (the K3 surfaces) and the two exceptional
  divisors `y` (birational to `S × ℙ¹`, the *target* exceptional divisor) and `x` (birational to
  `S' × ℙ¹`, the *source* exceptional divisor).
-/

